2. Foundational Axioms and Epistemic Commitments

Every discipline requires axioms that define its scope and limits. Synthetic Ecology rests on the following.

2.1 The Closed‑Biosphere Postulate

An artificial biosphere is a thermodynamically open but mass‑closed system maintained far from equilibrium. The total invariant mass vector of all life‑supporting elements is fixed:

\sum_{i=1}^{N} \mathbf{m}_i(t) = \mathbf{M}_{\text{total}} = \text{const.}

where \mathbf{m}_i(t) denotes the time‑varying mass distribution across physical, biological, and synthetic reservoirs.

2.2 The Ecological‑Entropy Imperative

Without active intervention, closed biological systems degrade. The Second Law, when applied to organized ecological information, demands that internal entropy production \sigma_{\text{gen}} will, if unchecked, drive the system toward homogenisation and loss of functional complexity. The biosphere architect’s central task is to counterbalance this tendency with structured negentropy injection.

2.3 Cybernetic Integration

Passive monitoring is insufficient. A latency gap between observation and intervention can permit catastrophic state transitions. The biosphere must therefore be fused with an autonomous cybernetic layer that functions as an artificial nervous system, modulating biological activity in real time to maintain the system within a predefined survival manifold.

2.4 The Inevitability and Directability of Evolution

In genetically bottlenecked, high‑radiation environments, evolutionary change is not a possibility but a certainty, proceeding at rates far exceeding terrestrial baselines. A frozen “climax” ecosystem is a dangerous fiction. The architect must therefore steer unavoidable genetic drift toward a family of pre‑validated, safe evolutionary attractors.

2.5 The Unknown‑Unknowns Axiom (Epistemic Humility)

The governing equations of a living biosphere, denoted by the abstract dynamics

\dot{\mathbf{x}} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u},\mathbf{w},t),

are themselves subject to evolutionary change, emergent phenomena, and non‑computable interactions. Any theoretical framework that treats \mathbf{f}(\cdot) as fixed and fully known will eventually fail. The programme therefore insists that evolutionary system identification—the continuous re‑estimation of the system’s changing laws—is as fundamental as control.

2.6 Civilisational Embeddedness

The survival manifold is not merely biochemical. Human psychology, culture, governance, and collective cognition are integral components of the system. A biosphere that satisfies oxygen and caloric requirements but destroys meaning, trust, or institutional continuity is not a successful biosphere; it is a pre‑collapse artefact.

