4. Programme Structure

The PhD is a four‑year, research‑intensive curriculum. Formal coursework occupies the first two years; the final two years are dedicated to original dissertation research.

Year 1 — Foundations of Synthetic Ecology

· Closed‑System Thermodynamics and Non‑Equilibrium Ecology
· Information Theory for Biological Networks
· Cybernetics and Autonomous Control Systems
· Evolutionary Dynamics and Bifurcation Theory
· Ecological Computation Theory (new core)
· Research Seminar: Historic Biosphere Failures and Their Mathematical Signatures

Year 2 — Advanced Domains and Qualifying Examination

· Mandatory modules:
  · Civilisational Ecological Risk Theory
  · Biosphere Constitutional Theory (seminar format)
· Elective modules (choose two):
  · Radiogenic Mutation Dynamics and Accelerated Evolution
  · Synthetic Microbial Consortia Engineering
  · Distributed AI Architectures for Decentralised Life Support
  · Human‑Ecological Psychology and the Civilisational Manifold
· Qualifying Examination: A formal defence of a novel theoretical construct (e.g., a new stability metric, a predictive drift model, a constitutional framework fragment, or a computational capacity theorem).
· Submission of dissertation proposal.

Years 3–4 — Doctoral Research

Independent research under a supervisory panel spanning ecology, control theory, computation, and social systems. Candidates select one of four dissertation tracks:

Track A — Mathematical Theory
New laws, proofs, or metrics. e.g., a rigorous proof that \Psi_e can be bounded by measurable genomic diversity indices; a theorem on minimal computational capacity for closed biospheres.

Track B — Synthetic Ecology
Design and simulation (or analogue experiment) of novel ecosystem architectures. e.g., a “dual‑attractor” biosphere that can safely oscillate between two distinct metabolic regimes after a perturbation.

Track C — Evolutionary Governance
Theory and modelling of adaptive biosphere governance. e.g., formal specification of a biosphere constitution with verifiable invariants and amendment procedures.

Track D — Civilisational Biospheres
Integration of human social systems into biosphere design at settlement scale. e.g., coupling a multi‑agent model of collective cognition to a metabolic‑network simulator to test \Psi_{cv}.

All dissertations must explicitly address the implications of their work for the Grand Unified Question.

