6. Core Theoretical Derivations

The following derivations represent foundational intellectual milestones. Candidates are expected to master them and, in their dissertations, to extend or challenge them.

6.1 Information‑Ecological Entropy Balance and Control Stability \Psi_c

We define the macro‑ecological configuration entropy:

H(X) = -\sum_i p_i \ln p_i, \quad p_i = \frac{\|\mathbf{m}_i\|}{\|\mathbf{M}_{\text{total}}\|}.

Physical thermodynamic entropy: S = S_{\max} - k_B I(X), with I(X) = H_{\max} - H(X). The entropy time derivative for an open system is:

\dot{S}(t) = \dot{S}_e(t) + \sigma_{\text{gen}}(t) - \gamma I(C;X),

where I(C;X) is the mutual information between the cybernetic controller C and the ecological state X, and \gamma is the efficiency of converting information into negentropy injection. At steady state (\dot{S}=0):

\gamma I(C;X) = \sigma_{\text{gen}} + \dot{S}_e.

The control stability index is therefore:

\Psi_c \equiv \frac{\gamma I(C;X)}{\sigma_{\text{gen}}}.

Limitation. \Psi_c measures only the real‑time information‑theoretic balance. It says nothing about whether the species network can structurally persist (\Psi_s), whether the genetic architecture will drift out of the viable envelope (\Psi_e), or whether human society will remain governable (\Psi_{cv}). A full certification requires all four indices.

6.2 Robust Lyapunov Stability Under Bounded Space Perturbations (Guaranteeing \Psi_c \ge 1)

We linearise the controlled ecosystem around a target equilibrium \mathbf{x}^* within \Omega_{\text{civilisation}}:

\dot{\mathbf{e}} = \mathbf{A}\mathbf{e} + \mathbf{B}\mathbf{u} + \mathbf{D}\mathbf{w}, \quad \|\mathbf{w}\| \le w_{\max},

with error \mathbf{e} = \mathbf{x} - \mathbf{x}^*. Choose Lyapunov function V(\mathbf{e}) = \mathbf{e}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{e}, \mathbf{P} > 0, satisfying \mathbf{A}^T\mathbf{P} + \mathbf{P}\mathbf{A} = -\mathbf{Q} for nominal stable \mathbf{A}_0, \mathbf{Q}>0.

Apply the robust control law:

\mathbf{u} = -\mathbf{K}\mathbf{e} - \rho \frac{\mathbf{B}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{e}}{\|\mathbf{B}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{e}\|}.

The time derivative becomes:

\dot{V} \le -\mathbf{e}^T \mathbf{Q}^* \mathbf{e} - 2\rho \|\mathbf{B}^T \mathbf{P} \mathbf{e}\| + 2 w_{\max} \|\mathbf{D}^T \mathbf{P}\| \|\mathbf{e}\|.

Choosing \rho \ge \frac{w_{\max}\|\mathbf{D}^T \mathbf{P}\|}{\sigma_{\min}(\mathbf{B}^T \mathbf{P})} + \delta with \delta>0 guarantees \dot{V}<0 outside a small ultimate bound \epsilon. The system is Input‑to‑State Stable (ISS), and \mathbf{x}(t) remains within a neighbourhood of \mathbf{x}^* entirely contained in \Omega_{\text{civilisation}} by design. Thus \Psi_c \ge 1 is achievable through appropriate cybernetic architecture, given known dynamics.

Caution. This proof assumes that \mathbf{A}, \mathbf{B}, and the very definition of \mathbf{x} are stationary. In a living biosphere, they are not. The extension of such proofs to the case of evolving system matrices is the subject of evolutionary system identification and remains an open research frontier.

