7. Assessment and Ethical‑Civilisational Anchoring

7.1 Doctoral Assessment Criteria

· Theoretical Originality: Has the dissertation established a new principle, proof, or architecture that advances the Grand Unified Question?
· Mathematical and Computational Rigour: Are all derivations fully formalised, and are the limits of applicability clearly stated?
· Epistemic Honesty: Does the work acknowledge the domains where controllability assumptions break down, and does it propose methods to cope with unknown unknowns?
· Civilisational Alignment: Does the work explicitly embed the values of resilience, intergenerational equity, and psychological flourishing into formal constraints?

7.2 The Ethical‑Constitutional Oath — Formalised

The traditional ethical oath is restated as a set of design invariants, which candidates must render as unviolable constraints in their models:

I recognise that every living system entrusted to my care supports future generations. I will design ecosystems that prioritise resilience over convenience, sustainability over short‑term gain, and life over efficiency. I will respect biological complexity, acknowledge the limits of my models, and build constitutional safeguards that prevent optimisation from overriding the conditions in which civilisation—and all life—can flourish.

In algorithmic form, the Constitutional Invariants include, at minimum:

\mathbf{x}(t) \in \Omega_{\text{civilisation}} \quad \forall t,

\mathcal{D}_j(\mathbf{x},t) \ge \mathcal{D}_{j,\min} \quad \text{(biodiversity floors)},

\Psi_s, \Psi_e, \Psi_c, \Psi_{cv} \ge 1 \quad \text{(all stability dimensions certified)}.

